# libretranslate
msgid "`FORCE_CANCEL_AFTER_SECONDS` turns the cancel into an escalation. A cancel request does not stop a running step: the runner asks it to end, and a step that ignores the signal keeps going until its own `timeout-minutes`, which is 350 for a deploy. The concurrency group holds the newer run on `pending` until the occupant is *terminal*, not until it is cancel-requested, so a superseded sweep can block its branch for hours after being told to stop -- measured at eight minutes and counting, with ten deploy jobs untouched. After the wait, every run that is still not `completed` gets [`force-cancel`](https://docs.github.com/en/rest/actions/workflow-runs), which bypasses the conditions that keep it alive. The skipped cleanup steps are the price, and for a run that has already been superseded its artefacts are of no use to anyone. Leave the variable unset to cancel and walk away, which is what a closed pull request and a deleted branch want."
msgstr "`FORCE_CANCEL_AFTER_SECONDS` omdanner annulleringen til en eskalering. En annullerings anmodning stopper ikke et kørende trin: runner beder det om at afslutte, og et trin, der ignorerer signalet fortsætter indtil sin egen `timeout-minutes`, som er 350 til en udsendelse. Concurrency gruppen holder den nyere køre på `pending` indtil brugeren er *terminal*, ikke før det er aflyst-ønsket, så en erstattet sweep kan blokere sin gren i timer efter at have fået at vide at stoppe -- målt på otte minutter og tælle, med ti implementere job uberørt. Efter ventetiden, hver løb, der stadig ikke `completed` får [`force-cancel`](https://docs.github.com/en/rest/actions/workflow-runs), som omgår de betingelser, der holder det i live. De sprunget oprydning trin er den pris, og for en kørsel, der allerede er blevet erstattet sine artefakter er til ingen nytte for nogen. Efterlad variablen frakoblet for at annullere og gå væk, hvilket er hvad en lukket pull anmodning og en slettet gren ønsker."

